==English==
{{was wotd|2008|January|21}}

===Etymology===
[[Image:Bastion (PSF).jpg|thumb|A bastion (1)]]
{{wikipedia}}
First attested in 1562. From {{der|en|fr|bastion}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}} {{m|fr|bastille||fortress}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|/ˈbæsti.ən/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/ˈbæsti.ən/|/ˈbæstʃən/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-bastion.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}, {{audio|en-us-bastion-2.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|Bastian}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# a [[projecting]] part of a [[rampart]] or other [[fortification]]
#* '''1942''', {{w|Emily Carr}}, ''{{w|The Book of Small}}'', "Beginnings," [http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks04/0400201.txt]
#*: {{...}} Fort Camosun had swelled herself from being a little Hudson's Bay Fort, inside a stockade with '''bastions''' at the corners, into being the little town of Victoria, and the capital of British Columbia.
# a well-[[fortified]] [[position]]; a [[stronghold]] or [[citadel]]
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} a person, or thing, who strongly [[defend]]s some [[principle]]

